VoltRelay Energy: Operations & Customer Experience Analysis

1. Problem Understanding
VoltRelay Energy has experienced substantial growth across its six-city EV battery-swapping network, but this scale has masked deteriorating operational health.
Despite increasing total swaps and revenue, the network is suffering from rising service failures, eroding per-swap margins, and declining new-rider retention.
Leadership requires an evidence-based diagnosis of these friction points to determine whether the upcoming operating budget should be deployed toward geographic
expansion, deeper battery inventory, network-wide pricing changes, or fleet partner exclusivity.

2. Analytical Approach
The investigation leveraged 18 months of operational history encompassing 3.9 million swap events, 20,000 rider profiles, and station telemetry.
The analytical pipeline included:
Data Integrity Resolution: Corrected a timestamp logging error linked to firmware v3.2.0, deduplicated offline-batch sync anomalies, and standardized geographic market naming conventions.
KPI Definition: Redefined rider churn at the individual user level (30 days of inactivity) rather than the transaction level to eliminate survivorship bias.
Hypothesis Testing: Systematically isolated and tested operational variables, comparing outcomes across vehicle classes (2W vs. 3W), commercial arrangements (Fleet vs. Independent), geographic markets, and equipment generations.

3. Key Insights
The Mealtime Bottleneck: While the network boasts a 94.04% top-line success rate, this average conceals over 200,000 critical failures (failed_no_charged_battery and abandoned_queue). 
These failures are highly concentrated during peak gig-economy earning windows: the 12:00 PM lunch rush and the 7:00 PM - 9:00 PM evening commute, where network outages exceed 25,000 per hour. 
The Wait Time Cliff: When the network bottlenecks, riders face an average queue wait time of 11.71 minutes before abandoning the attempt. For logistics and quick-commerce riders, 
this idle time directly destroys their daily earning potential. 
The Gen1 Hardware Deficit: The root cause of these bottlenecks is localized hardware performance. Gen1 charging cabinets fail at a rate of 6.51%, severely underperforming newer 
Gen2 (4.11%) and Gen3 (4.10%) infrastructure. Gen1 stations are responsible for 118,531 absolute failures—more than all newer equipment combined.
Universal Churn: The platform suffers a massive ~40% true churn rate. This attrition is uniform across all cities (37% - 42%), 
vehicle classes (39.4% for 2W, 41.5% for 3W), and rider segments (40.0% for Fleet, 39.2% for Independent).

