5. Business Findings
Rider attrition is not a pricing, geographic, or battery degradation problem. It is a predictable outcome of severe hardware bottlenecks during peak operating hours. 
The Gen1 charging cabinets cannot process depleted batteries fast enough to support the throughput required by VoltRelay's core user base. Because the resulting 12-minute
wait times and complete stockouts occur uniformly across the network during crucial earning windows, gig workers of all types are abandoning the platform at a 40% rate.

Network-wide pricing pilots failed to curb this attrition because price adjustments do not resolve the physical charging limitations of the hardware. 
Similarly, the data indicates that expanding the footprint without addressing Gen1 turnaround times will only replicate a broken operational model in new zip codes.

6. Actionable Recommendations
Primary Budget Allocation (More Batteries): Immediately deploy the upcoming operating budget to purchase and inject heavy battery inventory into all high-density transit hubs utilizing Gen1 equipment. 
This localized inventory expansion will create a deep physical buffer to absorb the lunch and dinner demand spikes that the older chargers cannot keep up with.
CapEx Reallocation (Gen1 Retrofits): Evaluate the cost-benefit of retrofitting existing Gen1 cabinets with Gen3 internals. If the cost of localized battery buffering exceeds 
the cost of a hardware upgrade over a 12-month horizon, shift capital expenditure to station modernization.

Halt Geographic Expansion: Suspend the Wave 3 station expansion plan. Focus all operational resources on stabilizing existing network reliability and 
driving the 40% churn rate down to a sustainable baseline (<20%) before attempting to scale the platform's footprint.
